        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: చక్కెర",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "చక్కెర (cakkera) — \"sugar\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "చక్కెర (cakkera) is sugar, and the word is a traveller. Sanskrit śarkarā meant \"grit, gravel\" — the stuff arrived in grains — and the same word went west through Persian and Arabic and Latin until English got \"sugar\" out of it. The Telugu shape and the English one are cousins with a very long road between them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu also keeps బెల్లం, the unrefined block of palm or cane sugar, for everything traditional. చక్కెర (cakkera) is the white refined thing that came later."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A pinch of చక్కెర (cakkera) goes into dishes an English kitchen would leave savoury, and it goes in to round off the salt rather than to make anything sweet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four."
            }
